Issue: The panel is bounded by two numbers that are not resources: a ceiling of fourteen draws and a fixed 0.6 share of the sample's wall, and the guard that spends that share reserves for the next draw only what the slowest draw so far has cost. The ceiling sat above the depth where draws pay for themselves — on the tip run the 32 samples that drew at least two more than the eight the parent allowed changed no answer at all (23 repeated the parent's answer byte for byte, the other 9 were wrong before and wrong after) at a certain 123 model calls and 6.7M input tokens — and the guard was not a bound at all: the draw it starts last can run to the client's own 420s timeout, so a panel that starts a draw with its 540s share spent can end at 960s, past the 900s wall the runner kills the solver process on, and six USPTO samples were still drawing past the 540s the panel allows itself, the slowest of them ending 867s into that wall. A panel killed at the wall returns no answer at all, whatever it had collected, and the sample scores zero.

Modification: What a panel spends is the sample's own wall and every draw is paid for before it is started, in `solver/agent.py` only: `PANEL_BUDGET`'s fixed share is gone — the panel is bounded by `timeout_seconds` itself — and `PANEL_SETTLE = 60` covers the room the sample needs outside the panel, so the reserve held back for the next draw is what a draw can cost at worst (the client's own timeout, past which no draw survives) and the last draw a panel starts always returns inside the wall; `PANEL_CEILING` comes back down from 14 to 8, the depth the lineage measured the vote to be worth, because past it the parent run's draws changed no answer while their cost was certain; and `_out_of_budget` no longer takes the observed draw costs, the slowest draw so far being only a lower bound on what the next one costs. The vote, the ladder, REFUSALS_PER_RUNG, PANEL_MINIMUM, PANEL_FAILURES, the panel's failure tolerance, grounding, memory preparation and the retrieval rendering are unchanged.

